Higher order buys more digits for the same h, if the function is smooth enough for the Taylor argument to hold.

Introduce the notation slowly. O(h^p) means: for small h, the error is at most a constant times h^p. It says nothing about the size of C, and nothing about where “small” starts. Numerical analysis uses O() in two directions: for errors, h goes to 0 and a higher power is better; for cost (second half of the lecture), n goes to infinity and a higher power is worse. Warn students about that now; it trips everybody.

The condition number of a problem
A problem: compute y = f(x). Nudge the input by a small relative amount. By Taylor, f(x + \Delta x) \approx f(x) + f'(x)\,\Delta x, so
\underbrace{\frac{|\Delta y|}{|y|}}_{\text{rel. change out}}
\approx \underbrace{\left|\frac{x\, f'(x)}{f(x)}\right|}_{\kappa(x)}
\cdot \underbrace{\frac{|\Delta x|}{|x|}}_{\text{rel. change in}}

\kappa(x), the (relative) condition number, is the amplification factor for relative errors.
\kappa \approx 1: errors pass through unchanged. Well-conditioned.
\kappa \gg 1: errors are magnified. Ill-conditioned.
\kappa belongs to f and x, the mathematical problem. No algorithm appears in it.


This is a one-line Taylor argument (first-order Taylor polynomial from the intro unit): change in output is roughly derivative times change in input. Divide both sides by y, multiply and divide by x, and the ratio of relative changes is |x f’(x) / f(x)|. Stress the last bullet: it is computed from the exact function, before any computer is involved. That is what makes it a property of the problem.

By the end of this exercise you should be able to:

say what a numerical method is, and name the three ways a computation goes wrong: rounding, truncation, instability;
classify a failure you meet in practice into one of the three;
write down a Taylor polynomial \(P_n\) and use the remainder term to bound its error, by hand;
read the order of an approximation from errors at halved steps, and from the slope of a log–log plot;
see that more work buys accuracy only down to a rounding floor;
detect a fit that converged to a wrong answer, with a plot, several starting values and a units check.

The by-hand problems are the core of this unit. The code problems confirm them and show the same ideas at a size where only code works.

